          "review_rationale": "The original second discrete-time martingale variant states its process (X_n,F_n) is integrable, which means E|X_n|<∞ for each n≥0. The first discrete-time and continuous-time martingale formulations explicitly state E|X_t|<∞ at each index, without requiring a common bound, and the conditional-expectation source also uses integrability as finite absolute expectation. The sole shared prerequisite is coordinatewise L¹ integrability for a real random variable at each time parameter; specializing its index set T from nonnegative reals to nonnegative integers gives precisely the discrete-time condition. The process index set, adaptation, filtration, conditional expectation identities, and absence of a uniform L¹ bound remain specified in their respective full source formulations."
